1. What is CSS?
Interview answer:
CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. It is used to style and design HTML web pages.

Key points:
CSS controls colors, fonts, spacing, layout, etc.
CSS separates presentation from HTML structure.
🧠 Trick:
HTML = Structure | CSS = Style | JS = Behavior


2. What are the different ways to apply CSS?
There are 3 ways:
| Type     | Where?               | Example                   |
| -------- | -------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Inline   | Inside HTML element  | `style="color:red"`       |
| Internal | Inside `<style>`     | `<style>...</style>`      |
| External | Separate `.css` file | `<link rel="stylesheet">` |

3. What is the CSS Syntax?
selector {
    property: value;
}

Example:
p {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 20px;
}
p → selector
color → property
blue → value


4. What is a CSS Selector?
A selector is used to select HTML elements that you want to style.
Common selectors:

p { }
.box { }
#header { }
* { }


⭐ 5. What are the types of CSS selectors?
Important selectors:
| Selector  | Example              | Selects             |
| --------- | -------------------- | ------------------- |
| Universal | `*`                  | All elements        |
| Element   | `p`                  | All `<p>`           |
| Class     | `.box`               | Elements with class |
| ID        | `#header`            | Element with ID     |
| Group     | `h1, p`              | Multiple elements   |
| Attribute | `input[type="text"]` | Matching attributes |

⭐ 6. What is the difference between Class and ID in CSS?
| Class             | ID                                 |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `.box`            | `#box`                             |
| Can be reused     | Should identify one unique element |
| Lower specificity | Higher specificity                 |

🧠 Trick:
. = Class | # = ID

⭐ 7. What is CSS Specificity?
Specificity determines which CSS rule wins when multiple rules target the same element.
Basic order:
Inline > ID > Class/Attribute/Pseudo-class > Element

Example:
p {
    color: red;
}

.text {
    color: blue;
}

#title {
    color: green;
}

If an element has all three, the ID rule has higher specificity than class and element.

⭐ 8. What is the CSS Box Model?
| Part    | Meaning              |
| ------- | -------------------- |
| Content | Actual content       |
| Padding | Space inside border  |
| Border  | Boundary             |
| Margin  | Space outside border |

⭐ 9. Padding vs Margin
| Padding                            | Margin                                   |
| ---------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Space inside the element           | Space outside the element                |
| Between content and border         | Between element and surrounding elements |
| Background can extend into padding | Background doesn't extend into margin    |

Example:
.box {
    padding: 20px;
    margin: 20px;
}

⭐ 10. What is box-sizing?
It controls how an element's width and height are calculated.
content-box
Default behavior.

Width applies to content only.
border-box

Width includes:
Content + Padding + Border

Example:

* {
    box-sizing: border-box;
}

⭐ 11. What is the difference between display: block, inline, and inline-block?
| Block                         | Inline                                         | Inline-block          |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| New line                      | Same line                                      | Same line             |
| Usually takes available width | Takes content width                            | Takes specified width |
| Width/height works            | Width/height generally don't apply as expected | Width/height works    |

⭐ 12. What is display: none?
It removes the element from the layout.

.box {
    display: none;
}

The element does not occupy space.
Difference from visibility: hidden
.box {
    visibility: hidden;
}

The element is invisible but still occupies space.
🧠 Trick:
display:none = Gone | visibility:hidden = Invisible

⭐ 13. What is the position property?
It controls how an element is positioned.
Main values:

static
relative
absolute
fixed
sticky

| Value      | Simple meaning                                      |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| `static`   | Normal position                                     |
| `relative` | Moves relative to its normal position               |
| `absolute` | Positioned relative to a containing block           |
| `fixed`    | Fixed relative to viewport                          |
| `sticky`   | Acts relative until a scroll threshold, then sticks |



⭐ 14. Relative vs Absolute
Relative
.box {
    position: relative;
    top: 10px;
}

Moves relative to its normal position.

Absolute
.box {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
}

Removed from normal flow and positioned relative to its positioned containing block.

🧠 Trick:
Relative = Own position | Absolute = Containing block

⭐ 15. What is z-index?
z-index controls the stacking order of positioned elements and certain other stacking contexts.

Higher value generally appears in front when elements participate in the same stacking context.

.box1 {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 10;
}

🧠 Trick:
z-index = Front/Back

⭐ 16. What is Flexbox?
Flexbox is a CSS layout system designed mainly for one-dimensional layouts.
It works with:
Row
Column

Example:
.container {
    display: flex;
}

Important properties:

flex-direction
justify-content
align-items
flex-wrap
gap


⭐ 17. justify-content vs align-items
This is a very common interview question.
The answer depends on the flex direction.
For default:
flex-direction: row;
| Property          | Controls   |
| ----------------- | ---------- |
| `justify-content` | Main axis  |
| `align-items`     | Cross axis |

.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

⭐ 18. What is CSS Grid?
CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system.
It works with:
Rows + Columns

Example:
.container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
}

🧠 Trick:
Flex = 1D | Grid = 2D

⭐ 19. Flexbox vs Grid
| Flexbox                         | Grid                   |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| One-dimensional                 | Two-dimensional        |
| Row OR column                   | Rows AND columns       |
| Great for components/navigation | Great for page layouts |


⭐ 20. What are pseudo-classes?
Pseudo-classes define a special state of an element.

Examples:
a:hover {
    color: red;
}

input:focus {
    border: 2px solid blue;
}

Common ones:

:hover
:focus
:active
:visited
:first-child
:last-child
:nth-child()



⭐ 21. What are pseudo-elements?
Pseudo-elements style a specific part of an element.

Examples:
p::first-letter {
    font-size: 30px;
}
p::before {
    content: "→ ";
}

Common:
::before
::after
::first-letter
::first-line


⭐ 22. What are CSS units?
Absolute unit
px
Relative units
%
em
rem
vw
vh
| Unit  | Based on                                                 |
| ----- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| `px`  | Fixed CSS pixel unit                                     |
| `%`   | Relative to containing context                           |
| `em`  | Relative to font size of relevant element/parent context |
| `rem` | Root element's font size                                 |
| `vw`  | Viewport width                                           |
| `vh`  | Viewport height                                          |

⭐ 23. em vs rem
| `em`                                                     | `rem`                               |
| -------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Relative to relevant inherited/current font-size context | Relative to root (`html`) font size |
| Can compound through nesting                             | More predictable                    |

Example:
p {
    font-size: 2rem;
}

🧠 Trick:
REM = Root EM

⭐ 24. What is responsive web design?
Responsive design means a website adapts to different screen sizes.

Examples:
Mobile
Tablet
Laptop
Desktop

Common tools:

Media queries
Flexible layouts
Flexbox
Grid
Responsive units

⭐ 25. What are Media Queries?
Media queries apply CSS based on conditions such as viewport size.

Example:
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    .container {
        flex-direction: column;
    }
}

26. What is overflow?
Controls what happens when content doesn't fit inside an element.

Values:
visible
hidden
scroll
auto

Example:
.box {
    overflow: hidden;
}

27. What is the difference between opacity: 0 and display: none?
| `opacity: 0`                                          | `display: none`             |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Element becomes transparent                           | Element removed from layout |
| Still occupies space                                  | Doesn't occupy space        |
| Can still receive pointer events depending on context | Not rendered                |

| `opacity: 0`                                          | `display: none`             |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Element becomes transparent                           | Element removed from layout |
| Still occupies space                                  | Doesn't occupy space        |
| Can still receive pointer events depending on context | Not rendered                |

28. What is inherit?
It tells a CSS property to take its value from the parent element when the property supports inheritance.

p {
    color: inherit;
}

30. What is the difference between relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky?
| Position   | Remember                                |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `relative` | Relative to normal position             |
| `absolute` | Relative to containing block            |
| `fixed`    | Fixed to viewport                       |
| `sticky`   | Sticks during scrolling after threshold |

